#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e651cb4-ae24-5ac7-9c01-d90de562b155" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "876c3c8e-19f9-56b2-a451-3c2578ba7af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "876c3c8e-19f9-56b2-a451-3c2578ba7af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511061} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8bd0d4-e2df-5b96-ba24-b2e56674f0f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-166362" :question/problem "From the given data, the best forward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{120-59}{1} \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a forward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $61$ customers per month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9593a22e-5aa8-5e2e-b0d9-870be17ad1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9593a22e-5aa8-5e2e-b0d9-870be17ad1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511091} :answer-field/id #uuid "74caf0bf-cacd-5f28-aa72-d801b776ea5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242" :question/math-academy-id "q-166363" :question/problem "From the given data, the best backward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." :question/worked-solution "Again, we use the fact that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1)-q(1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(1)-q(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{59-0}{1} \\\\  & =59.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a backward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $59$ customers per month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32acdae7-2eaa-5d7b-bea5-1d4a19711cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32acdae7-2eaa-5d7b-bea5-1d4a19711cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510891} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff10b37-1425-5f08-9969-ac5f0106b371" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845" :question/math-academy-id "q-166364" :question/problem "Using a trapezoidal sum with two equal subintervals, ${\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈${{field-1}}\n." :question/worked-solution "The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(a)+2(q({t}_{1})+q({t}_{2})+…+q({t}_{n-1}))+q(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n* The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n* The corresponding interval is $[0,4],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=4.$\n\n* The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-0}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(0)+2q(2)+q(4)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(120)+108]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240+108 \\\\  & =348.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064267 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68635d27-a9ef-541b-a57b-d9f5f422ae85" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a73ce7dc-ebea-528a-9250-92701e8d78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a73ce7dc-ebea-528a-9250-92701e8d78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511136} :answer-field/id #uuid "5847b751-f98d-563e-aaf4-cc2c04b1208d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237" :question/math-academy-id "q-166365" :question/problem "Use your answer to part 3 to approximate the average number of customers at the campsite during the first $4$ months.\n\nThe average number of customers at the campsite over the first four months was\n{{field-1}}\n." :question/worked-solution "The average value of a continuous function $q(t)$ on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈348.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}≈\\frac{348}{4-0}=87.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adf1cf9-8c4d-5665-8948-53a09c6c23e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adf1cf9-8c4d-5665-8948-53a09c6c23e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510871} :answer-field/id #uuid "129d9d3d-517b-56d8-9503-4a5fa301d52f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-166366" :question/problem "For $0\\le t\\le 8,$ the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least{{field-1}}\nsolution(s)." :question/worked-solution "The solutions to ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ correspond to the stationary points of the graph of $q(t).$\n\nSketching a possible graph of $q(t),$ we get the following:\n\n![](images/2f/2fd9e08520f4a671a255f360e583140175650de856a12233962c99a064c4c582.png)\n\nFrom our sketch, we notice three stationary points located near $t=2,$ $t=3,$ and $t=4.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least three solutions."}]}
